## A B S T R A C T
Here, we developed chitosan (CS)-based nanofibrous scaffold consisting of tannic acid (TA) and zinc-based metalorganic framework (MOF) as a novel antibacterial and hemostatic wound dressing. The effect of MOF content and its incorporation within and onto CS/PVA-TA nanofibrous scaffolds were studied. The morphological characterization of fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds revealed the formation of uniform and bead-free nanofibers with an average diameter between 120 and $150\mathrm{nm}$ . The uniform and continuous decoration of MOF crystals on nanofibrous scaffold surfaces were confirmed by FESEM. The developed nanofibrous scaffolds exhibit appro­ priate physicochemical characteristics such as chemical and crystalline structure, surface wettability and swelling, and mechanical properties. It is shown that the incorporation of TA and MOFs greatly enhanced the hemostatic performance of the CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffold by providing rapid liquid absorbability and accel­ erating the aggregation of coagulation factors and platelets. Furthermore, the results of the MTT assay suggested the good biocompatibility of nanofibrous scaffolds containing MOF nanoparticles. The nanofibrous scaffolds exhibited excellent antibacterial activity against Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus. The disk diffusion antibacterial assay showed that the nanofibrous scaffolds containing TA and MOF could protect wound from bacterial infection. The findings provide new insights to develop a MOF-modified nanofibrous structure with great potential for hemostatic wound dressing application.
## 1. Introduction
Uncontrollable bleeding has been considered as one of the most leading causes of death around the world [1,2]. This problem becomes more challenging when the inherent hemostatic system of the body fails to stop bleeding quickly. Utilization of standard gauze dressings and applying direct pressure are also not adequate for irregularly shaped wounds with uncontrollable bleeding [3,4]. Thus, the development of new wound dressing materials with enhanced hemostatic properties is quite necessary. Meanwhile, the bacterial infection is a crucial issue that can impair wound healing, as the blood clot can become a hotbed of bacterial growth and cause wound inflammation [3]. In the past de­ cades, many wound dressing materials have been applied for different wound types for promoting hemostatic activity as well as protecting them from infection [5–8]. However, some limitations such as the need for the addition of antimicrobial agents and/or losing moisturizing effect may limit their wide applications [9]. Hence, there has been an increasing demand for the development of novel multi-functional wound dressings with rapid and effective hemostatic and antibacterial
activity.
Naturally-derived materials such as chitosan [10], collagen [11], fibrinogen [12], fibrin [13], cellulose [14], alginate [15], and gelatin [5] have been widely used for hemostatic dressings. Among these materials, chitosan (CS), as a natural polymer provided by deacetylation of chitin, has been abundantly used as wound dressings. The non-toxicity, biocompatibility, antimicrobial activity, and hydrophilicity of chitosan allow its application in several areas, especially in the fabrication of wound dressings. Despite the privileges of CS, it has some drawbacks such as limited blood absorption in excessive amounts and antibacterial activity in solution, which make its application on wound dressings rather difficult [3,16]. Moreover, the fabrication of CS nanofibrous scaffolds, as one of the major forms of hemostatic dressings, is quite difficult. To overcome this obstacle, several easily electrospinnable polymers, such as poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA), poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO), and so on, have been suggested to blend with CS. Fabrication of core-sheath nanofibers using coaxial electrospinning and pressurized gyration is another approach for overcoming this deficiency [17–19].
Tannic acid (TA) is a naturally derived polyphenol with a relatively high molecular weight that can interact with carbohydrates and bio­ logical macromolecules [20,21]. TA possesses several favorable features such as antioxidant activity, biocompatibility, hemostatic and antibac­ terial properties that make it attractive as wound dressings. Therefore, a combination of TA with other materials like CS is expected to syner­ gistically provide much better healing properties.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as relatively emerging crystalline nanoporous materials, have been gaining many interests thanks to their large specific surface area, high porosity, adjustable pore size, versatile functionality, and enriched host-guest chemistry [22–25]. These fea­ tures make the MOFs favorable platforms for many applications such as water splitting [26], gas storage [27], catalysis [28], pollutant adsorp­ tion [29,30], health care [31,32], and particularly the design of biocompatible and biodegradable wound healing system [33,34]. Furthermore, the unstable coordination of metal ions with ligands en­ dows intrinsic biodegradability and good biocompatibility [35,36]. Many studies have revealed the beneficial effect of MOFs in wound dressings. For instance, hemostasis and anti-infection properties of chitin sponge modified with zinc MOF has been demonstrated [37]. In another study, the Cu-MOF incorporated CS/PVA nanofibrous mem­ brane exhibited an enhanced antibacterial property [38].
The successful demonstration of antibacterial and wound healing properties of TA and CS-based nanofibers as well as the unique char­ acteristics of the reported MOFs inspired us to look into the possibility of fabricating new structures by introducing the zinc-based MOF (ZIF-8) and TA to CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffolds. As there is no report in the open literature about such structures, this study aims to design and fabricate a novel hemostatic wound dressing based on MOFincorporated CS nanofibers. The morphological and structural proper­ ties along with preliminary in-vitro analysis of the prepared nanofibrous wound dressings were investigated. The antibacterial activity of pro­ vided nanofibrous scaffolds against the gram-negative bacterium Escherichia coli and the gram-positive bacterium Staphylococcus aureus is shown. According to the results, the provided MOF-incorporated CS nanofibrous scaffolds have a high potential to be used for wound dressings.
## 2. Experimental procedure
## 2.1. Materials
Chitosan (CS) powder (medium molecular weight), polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, $\mathrm{1w}\quad=\quad60,000\quad\mathrm{g/mol)}$ , zinc nitrate hexahydrate ((Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}).6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ purity), 2-methyl imidazole (2-MIM), tannic acid (TA), methanol (MeOH), glutaraldehyde (GA), and acetic acid were obtained from Merck Co. (Germany) and used as received without further purification. Deionized (DI) water was used throughout the experiment.
## 2.2. Synthesis of MOF nanoparticles
Zeolite imidazole framework (ZIF-8) nanoparticles were synthesized according to the procedure reported in the literature [39,40] with slight modification. Briefly, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}).6H_{2}O}$ $(4~\mathrm{g})$ and 2-MIM $(8~\ g)$ were dis­ solved separately in $150\mathrm{mLMeOH}$ followed by stirring for $3\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the 2-MIM solution was added to zinc nitrate solution at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under mild stirring conditions. After forming white precipitates, the product was collected by centrifuge, washed two times with MeOH, and finally dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3. Fabrication of nanofibrous scaffolds
The CS powder was dissolved in the acetic acid $(7\%\mathrm{v/v)}$ and gently stirred for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature. Similarly, the PVA dry powder was dissolved in DI water and stirred for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After cooling down the PVA solution to room temperature, two solutions were mixed together and stirred for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to reach a homogeneous CS/PVA solution. A specific amount $(1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ of tannic acid was added to the CS solution followed by adding PVA solution for the fabrication of tannic acid modified CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffold denoted CS/PVA-T. Afterward, different amounts of ZIF nanoparticles were added to CS/PVA-T solution and sonicated for 2 min using a probe-type sonicator (UHP-400, Ultrasonic Technology Development Co., Iran) to reach CS/PVA-TZ solutions including 1, 2, 3, and $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF nanoparticles. The viscosity of electrospinning solutions was measured at room temperature with a Brookfield viscometer (DV-E viscometer, Brookfield, USA). The details about the sample preparation have been listed in Table 1.
The prepared solutions were electrospun using a double nozzle electrospinning apparatus (Fanavaran Nano Meghyas Co., Iran) utilizing a plastic syringe, a DC high voltage power supply, and a grounded aluminum collecting drum with traverse mode. Electrospinning of chitosan-based solutions was carried out at a voltage of $25\mathrm{~kV}$ , a spin­ ning distance of $17\ \mathrm{cm}$ , a volume flow rate of $0.6~\mathrm{mL/h}$ , and a rotating drum speed of $150~\mathrm{rpm}$ . All experiments were performed at room tem­ perature. These conditions were obtained based on CS/PVA solution in our previous study [41] with slight modification and applied to all samples.
## 2.4. In-situ growth of MOF on nanofibrous scaffold
The ZIF-8 nanoparticles were deposited solvothermally on the crosslinked nanofibrous scaffolds. First, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}).6H_{2}O}$ $\left(1\ g\right)$ and 2- MIM $_{(2\mathrm{~g)}}$ were dissolved and mixed separately in $50\ \mathrm{mL}$ MeOH. Then, the 2-MIM solution was slowly poured into the zinc nitrate solu­ tion containing the CS/PVA-TZ5 nanofibrous scaffold at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under mild stirring conditions for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The final product (denoted as CS/ PVA-TZ@ZIF) was obtained after washing with methanol two times and drying at ambient temperature. Fig. 1 schematically shows the insitu synthesis process of MOF on nanofibrous scaffolds.
To crosslink the nanofibrous scaffolds, they were separately sub­ jected to glutaraldehyde (GA) vapor for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . The samples were kept in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and finally were washed with DI water twice for removal of residual crosslinking agent.
## 2.5. Characterizations
The size and morphologies of ZIF-8 nanoparticles and nanofibrous scaffolds were evaluated by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, MIRA III, TESCAN, Czech Republic). The ZIF-8 particle size and electrospun fiber diameter distribution were determined using Digim­ izer software by measuring the diameter of about 50 random particles and fibers in each image, respectively. The porosity of nanofibrous specimens was also obtained from an image analysis approach by employing the ImageJ $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ software. The elemental composition of fabri­ cated samples was investigated using energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy. The chemical structure of synthesized MOF and fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds were analyzed using Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) and attenuated total reflection (ATR-FTIR) spectroscopy by AVATAR FTIR instrument (Thermo Nicolet, USA). The X-ray diffraction (XRD, Philips PW 1730, Netherlands) using $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K\upalpha}$ radiation $\left(\lambda=0.154\right.$ nm) was employed to study the crystalline structure of synthesized MOF and nanofibrous scaffolds. To determine the specific surface area and pore volume of the synthesized ZIF-8 particles, the Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) and Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) methods were employed using a BELSORP-MINI II instrument (BEL Japan Inc.). Me­ chanical properties of nanofibrous membrane were investigated using an Instron-5566 universal testing machine (Instron, USA) with $50\mathrm{N}$ load cell according to ASTM D 882–10 standard. The tensile test was carried out triplicate for each, and then the average results were reported with standard deviation. To investigate the surface wettability of nanofibrous scaffolds, water contact angle measurement was conducted using optical contact angle device (CAG-20, Jikan Co., Iran). A $12~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ water droplet was dropped on the sample surface at room temperature and the optical image was taken after $\textbf{1}s$ . The average values of contact angle were determined from four measurements. The swelling capacity of nano­ fibrous scaffolds, as an important feature of wound dressings repre­ senting their ability to absorb wound exudates, was determined according to the following equation [9,42]:
$$
\mathit{S w e l l i n g\ r a t i o}=\frac{W_{s}-W_{d}}{W_{d}}\times100\
$$
where $W_{d}$ and $W_{s}$ are the weight of nanofibers before and after swelling in water after a period of time, respectively.
## 2.6. Blood clotting time test
To study the hemostatic performance of fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds, a blood clotting time (BCT) test was conducted. Whole blood was taken from a healthy human volunteer and immediately was placed in a tube with sodium citrate to prevent a premature start of the coag­ ulation process. $1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ nanofibrous specimens were added into a glass tube containing $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of blood with sodium citrate. After incu­ bation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ a specific amount of calcium chloride $\mathrm{(CaCl_{2})}$ solution was added to the tube to start coagulation. The tubes were gently in­ clined every $30\mathrm{~s~}$ to observe if the blood had coagulated. The blood clotting time was recorded when the blood completely lost its flow­ ability and adhered to the bottom of the tube. Three replication were performed for each specimen.
## 2.7. PT and aPTT assay
Prothrombin time (PT), activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) and also the international normalized ratio (INR) assays were performed to evaluate whether the fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds could activate the coagulation pathway for blood clotting. First, nanofibrous scaffolds $(1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ were added to $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ healthy human plasma and $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ thromboplastin. After incubation for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ calcium chloride $\mathrm{(CaCl_{2})}$ was added to the above mixture and the time to clot formation was measured using KC1 Delta coagulometer (Trinity Biotech Plc, Ireland) as aPTT. Similarly, for the PT assay, the nanofibrous scaf­ folds were added to the $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ healthy human plasma followed by the addition of $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ prothrombin solution. Then, the time to clot forma­ tion was measured as PT. All the measurements were carried out five times and the mean value was reported.
## 2.8. Antibacterial tests
## 2.8.1. Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)
The minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) values of nanofibrous scaffolds were determined using a micro-dilution method described in our previous publications [43,44]. The MIC was defined as the lowest concentration of antimicrobial agent inhibiting the visible growth of bacteria.
## 2.8.2. Antibacterial activity
The antibacterial activity of fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds against the Gram-positive bacteria, Staphylococcus aureus (ATCC 25923) and Gram-negative bacteria, Escherichia coli (ATCC 25922) was carried out using disk diffusion method. The bacterial suspensions with 0.5 McFarland turbidity standard (equivalent to $1.5\times\mathrm{{\bar{1}}0^{8}\mathrm{{CFU/mL}}},$ were cultured on Müller-Hinton (MH) agar plates by sterile swab. The spec­ imens were punched into $0.5\mathrm{cm}$ diameter disks and sterilized under UV light for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, they were placed directly in contact with the agar plates. After incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ , the diameter of the inhibition zone was measured.
## 2.9. MTT assay and cytotoxicity analysis
To study the toxicity of the nanofibrous scaffolds, the metabolic ac­ tivity of the cells was measured by a thiazolyl blue tetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay. The human fibroblast cells were obtained from the Iranian Biological Resource Center (Tehran, Iran). They were seeded in 96-well plates at a density of 10,000 cells/well and cultured in DMEM (Dul­ becco's Modified Eagle Medium), then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The nanofibrous scaffolds were sterilized and incubated with the culture medium for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the culture medium on the samples was replaced with $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ MTT solution and incubated again with this medium at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After removing the culture medium, dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}})$ was used to dissolve the formazan crystals in the cells using a shaker for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the optical density (OD) of formazan was measured at $570\mathrm{nm}$ by an ELISA reader (BioTek, USA) and the cell viability was calculated according to the following equation based on absorptions of a sample $(A_{s a m p l e})$ , blank $(A_{b l a n k})$ and control $(A_{c o n t r o l})$ [41,45]:
$$
C e l l\nu i a b i l i t y(\mathcal{Y})=\frac{A_{s a m p l e}-A_{b l a n k}}{A_{c o n t r o l}-A_{b l a n k}}\times100\
$$
## 2.10. Statistical analysis
Statistical analysis was carried out using the ANOVA test (one-way analysis of variance) with a $p$ -value $<0.01$ as statistically significant. The experiments were carried out in triplicate and the results were reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characteristics of MOF nanoparticles
Fig. 2a shows the FESEM micrograph of synthesized ZIF-8 particles. The result reveals the formation of uniform particles with well-defined morphology and smooth surface. The average particle size of these crystals is about $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ with uniform size distribution. The particle size distribution is shown in Fig. S1. Similar morphology has been reported for ZIF-8 particles in the literature [39]. The elemental composition of ZIF-8 by EDX (Fig. S2) confirmed the presence of Zn, O, and C atoms. The FTIR spectrum of ZIF-8 nanoparticles is shown in Fig. 2b. The peak at $3447\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ group of ZIF-8. The adsorption band at $1577~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ stretching. The methylene sym­ metric and asymmetric stretching modes appeared at 2851 and 2921 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , respectively. The vibration of the 2-MIM ring has appeared at $1350{-}1500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The out-of-plane and in-plane bending of 2-MIM are also observed in the range of $500{-}820~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $890{-}1340\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively [46]. The peak at $422~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is due to $\mathrm{{zn-N}}$ stretching vi­ bration [47]. The XRD pattern (Fig. 2c) exhibit crystalline structure with the main characteristic peaks of $2\uptheta=7.3^{\circ}$ , $10.3^{\circ}$ , $12.8^{\circ}$ , $14.8^{\circ}$ , $16.5^{\circ}$ , $18.1^{\circ}$ , $19.7^{\circ}$ , $22.2^{\circ}$ , $24.6^{\circ}$ , $25.7^{\circ}$ , $26.8^{\circ}$ , $28.9^{\circ}$ , and $29.8^{\circ}$ associated to the (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), (222), (123), (114), (233), (224), (134), (125), and (044) plane reflection, respectively. These peaks are matched with the characteristic peaks of ZIF-8. The same results have been reported by similar studies [40,48], authenticating the successful formation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles. The mean particle size $(D)$ of ZIF-8 crystals was determined using Scherrer's equation [49,50]:
$$
D=\frac{K\lambda}{\beta c o s\theta}
$$
where $K$ is the shape factor (0.94), $\lambda$ is the X-ray wavelength $(0.154\mathrm{nm})$ , $\beta$ is the full width at half maximum intensity (FWHM) in radian, and $\theta$ is the Bragg angle ${\bf\Pi}^{\left(^{\circ}\right)}$ . Here, the FWHM of the three characteristic diffraction peaks $(2\uptheta\:=\:7.3^{\circ}$ , $10.3^{\circ}$ , and $12.8^{\circ}\mathrm{:}$ was considered for calculation of average particle size. The results show the average particle size of ZIF-8 crystals is about $51.5\mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the SEM observation.
The nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherm of ZIF-8 nanoparticles was obtained to determine their specific surface area and porous structure (Fig. 2d). The results showed a typical type I isotherm at $77\mathrm{K},$ confirming the microporous structure of ZIF-8. The hysteresis loop at high relative pressure is attributed to the textural macroporosity, consistent with previous literature [51]. The specific surface area and total pore volume are found to be $850.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.49~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respec­ tively. The average pore diameter calculated from desorption is found to be $2.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , confirming the microporous structure of ZIF-8.
## 3.2. Characterization of nanofibrous scaffolds
## 3.2.1. Morphological study
The FESEM micrographs and size distribution of nanofibrous scaf­ folds are shown in Fig. 3. As can be seen, all the nanofibrous scaffolds have shown the bead-free uniform morphology with an average diam­ eter between 120 and $150~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The nanofibers have a smooth surface and uniform size distribution. Table 2 summarized comparison of the average diameter of electrospun nanofibers. Modification of CS/PVA nanofiber with TA leads to the increase of the average fiber diameter by ${\sim}5\%$ . This phenomenon could be attributed to the large number of hydroxyl groups of TA, forming hydrogen bonds with CS and PVA; thus, the viscosity of polymer solution was increased with the addition of TA, which lead to the slight increase of average diameter values [21,52]. The addition of ZIF nanoparticles into the CS/PVA solution resulted in the formation of almost uniform fibers. The ultrathin fibers with no bead formation and an average diameter of $140~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were obtained (Fig. 3c). Further investigation on increasing the ZIF content from 1 to $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ in electrospinning solution revealed no significant change in the average diameter of the CS/PVA-TZ nanofibrous scaffolds (Fig. S3).
Fig. 4 shows the FESEM images of the ZIF-coated nanofibrous scaf­ fold (CS/PVA-TZ $@$ ZIF). A large number of ZIF nanoparticles with hex­ agonal pyramid-like morphology were grown on the CS/PVA-TZ nanofibrous scaffold. The high-magnification FESEM image (Fig. 4b) showed that the uniform and continuous MOF crystals with an average particle size of about $80\mathrm{nm}$ were well inter-grown on the surface of the nanofibers. It is obvious that the spaces between the nanofiber bundles have been filled with ZIF nanoparticles. Moreover, it is found that MOF nanoparticles are tightly deposited on the nanofibrous scaffolds, as no peeling-off of particles upon thorough washing with methanol was observed. The same results have been reported for incorporation of different MOFs on electrospun nanofibers after several cycles growth [40,53], while here the nanofibrous scaffold was almost filled with ZIF crystals only after the first growth cycle. With increasing the growth time to $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , the size of MOF crystals increased from $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $240~\mathrm{nm}$ . The ZIF particles were grown uniformly and confined inside the nano­ fiber networks. Fig. S4 shows that the inner space of nanofibrous scaffolds was filled with well inter-grown large ZIF crystals. However, due to the low porosity and large particle size of ZIF-8, it was not suit­ able for further investigations in this study.
## 3.2.2. Structural analysis
The FTIR spectra of CS/PVA, CS/PVA-T, CS/PVA-Z, and CS/PVA-TZ5 nanofibrous scaffolds are shown in Fig. 5a. The broad absorption band at $3320~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ and $\tt N-H$ groups of CS and PVA. The absorption band at around $2919~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ stretching vibrations [54]. The peaks that appeared at 1728 and $1072\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the $\mathtt{C=}0$ bond and CO-C vibrations, respectively [55]. The peak at $1373\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is also related to the CO bond [40]. The vibration peak at $713~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for CS/PVA-T nanofibers is ascribed to the out-of-plane vibration of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ of the aro­ matic ring of TA. The out-of-plane vibration of the 2-MIM ligand in the ZIF-8 structure is also located below $800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . These peaks might overlap with each other in the CA/PVA-TZ spectrum.
The XRD patterns of nanofibrous scaffolds were obtained to inves­ tigate their crystalline structure (Fig. 5b). The results reveal the crys­ tallinity of nanofibrous scaffolds, which remained almost unchanged after the incorporation of TA and ZIF nanocrystals. The diffraction peaks of ZIF nanoparticles on nanofibrous composites were not so evident, due to the low weight fraction of ZIF in composite nanofibers as well as the formation of hydrogen bonding between the component of polymers [40].
## 3.2.3. Wettability of nanofibrous scaffolds
The wettability of nanofibrous scaffolds was investigated using water contact angle measurement and the results are shown in Fig. 6. All nanofibrous scaffolds exhibit high wettability with hydrophilic proper­ ties. The contact angle of CS/PVA nanofibers was found to be $45.3^{\circ}$ . For CS/PVA-T nanofibrous scaffold, the contact angle $(37.7^{\circ})$ was lower than that of CS/PVA nanofibers, which is attributed to a large number of hydrophilic hydroxyl groups of the TA molecules. A similar result has been reported, indicating that incorporation of TA leads to decreasing the contact angle of electrospun nanofibers [55]. The addition of TA as a crosslinking agent of chitosan had the same effect [56].
Further investigation on the wettability of MOF-incorporated nano­ fibrous scaffolds shows that CS/PVA-Z nanofibers become less hydro­ philic than CS/PVA with a contact angle of $46.5^{\circ}$ , presumably due to the increased roughness of scaffolds. This effect becomes more pronounced when ZIF nanocrystals were grown on the CS/PVA-TZ nanofibers sur­ face. The contact angle of the CS/PVA-TZ@ZIF nanofibrous scaffold was measured as $63.9^{\circ}$ , certifying lower hydrophilicity of MOF-incorporated nanofibers. This effect benefits the prevention of wound exudate leakage
[57].
## 3.2.4. Swelling behavior of nanofibrous scaffolds
The swelling ratio of nanofibrous scaffolds, as the characteristic of wound dressing especially in exudating wounds, was measured. Fig. 6c shows the swelling ratios of samples in water after $10~\mathsf{s}$ of the swelling process. The results show that the swelling ratio of CS/PVA nanofibers increased by the incorporation of TA. The CS/PVA-T nanofiber exhibited high capabilities in absorbing liquid compared to pristine CA/PVA nanofibers. TA molecule has hydrophilic hydroxyl groups which its presence in the nanofibrous structures increases their swelling degree. It is shown that the incorporation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles into CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffolds could beneficially increase the swelling ratio up to $312\%$ , which was consistent with the contact angle results. This enhanced property could be attributed to the high specific surface area and porous structure of ZIF-8 particles. It was observed that the swelling ratio of CA/PVA nanofibers was increased by about $30\%$ with the incorporation of both TA and ZIF-8. The obtained result reveals that fabricated CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffolds containing TA and ZIF-8 with a high swelling ratio have the potential to absorb wound exudates and to prevent the wound from the accumulation of exudates.
## 3.3. Mechanical properties of nanofibrous scaffolds
The mechanical properties of chitosan-based nanofibrous scaffolds were studied by tensile testing and the results are shown in Fig. 7. The representative tensile stress-strain curves (Fig. 7a) show that the CS/ PVA nanofibers turned to be more elastic after the incorporation of TA and ZIF-8 nanoparticles. It can be seen that the CS/PVA-T nanofibers have a significantly higher elongation at break and the same tensile strength compared to the CS/PVA nanofibers. The tensile strength, elongation at break, and toughness of nanofibrous scaffolds were also compared (Fig. 7b–d). The hydrogen bonding between TA and CS/PVA endows the CS/PVA-T nanofibers with high elongation and toughness. The CS/PVA-Z nanofibers possessed lower Young's Modulus and tensile strength than CS/PVA nanofibers, but higher elongation at break. The individual contribution of ZIF-8 on the load bearing and the mechanical strength of the CS/PVA nanofibers became less pronounced. Interest­ ingly, the simultaneous incorporation of TA and ZIF-8 (more than 2 wt $\%$ leads to enhanced tensile strength and elongation at break. The mechanical properties of the CS/PVA-TZ nanofibers improved markedly with increasing ZIF-8 content up to $3\mathrm{wt\%}$ , indicating a strong interfacial interaction between polymer chain and well dispersed ZIF-8. However, the tensile strength and Young's Modulus of CS/PVA-TZ5 nanofibers did not significantly different from CS/PVA-TZ3 nanofibers, which may possibly be due to the structural inhomogeneity induced by the aggre­ gation of nanoparticles at higher ZIF-8 content. Generally, the changes in the mechanical properties of the CS/PVA nanofiber after incorporation of TA and ZIF-8 are attributed to the formation of hydrogen bonds between CS/PVA chains and TA as well as the entan­ glement of the CS/PVA polymer chains in the porous structure of ZIF-8.
## 3.4. Hemostatic performance of nanofibrous scaffolds
## 3.4.1. Blood clotting time
A blood clotting time (BCT) test was carried out to determine the hemostasis performance of nanofibrous scaffolds. Fig. 8a, b shows the formation of a blood clot and the clotting time of the nanofibrous scaf­ fold and control sample. The results show that the BCT of all fabricated CS-based scaffolds was shorter than that of the control sample. The shortest clotting time was obtained by CS/PVA-T nanofibrous scaffold $(68\pm4s)$ . The incorporation of ZIF-8 to CS/PVA nanofibers also leads to shortening the BCT to $94\pm16s$ . The porous structure of ZIF-8 and the hydrophilic nature of fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds favor rapid blood absorption to concentrate the blood cells for bleeding stop [6]. More­ over, the gradual disintegration of ZIF-8 to positively charged zinc ions and 2-MIM, as a result of direct contact with blood, could also electro­ statically interact with red blood cells and consequently contribute to the formation of primary clots. The same results have been observed for ZIF-8 incorporated chitin composite sponge [37]. The findings of the BCT measurements confirmed the significant promotion of the devel­ oped nanofibrous scaffolds to blood coagulation.
## 3.4.2. Analysis of the coagulation mechanism
The coagulation pathway induced by chitosan-based nanofibrous scaffolds was also investigated by PT and aPTT assays. The PT is used to evaluate the extrinsic and common coagulation pathways, while the aPTT assesses the intrinsic pathway. As shown in Fig. 8c, PT was remarkably reduced by all the chitosan-based nanofibrous scaffolds compared with the control $(13.6~s)$ . The PT value of composite nano­ fibers containing TA and ZIF-8 $(5.02\pm0.45\mathrm{~s})$ ) was smaller than that of CS/PVA nanofibers $(5.12\pm0.24s)$ . However, no significant change in PT was observed for CS/PVA-T, CS/PVA-Z, and CS/PVA-TZ, suggesting that the composite nanofibrous scaffolds have the same hemostatic ef­ fect. Considering the INR values (Table 3), as the normalized pro­ thrombin time, the incorporation of TA and ZIF-8 into CS/PVA nanofibers could also favor the hemostatic mechanism. It was reported that the porous structures could enhance the hemostatic performance by providing rapid liquid absorbability and accelerating the aggregation of coagulation factors and platelets. The nanofibrous scaffolds can activate the coagulation factor VII, leading to fibrin formation. On the contrary to PT, the results of aPTT assay (Fig. 8d) did not show a significant change. These results revealed that the fabricated nanofibrous scaffolds participate in blood coagulation through the extrinsic pathway.
## 3.5. Cytotoxicity of nanofibrous scaffolds
The cytotoxicity of nanofibrous scaffolds and viability of cells was assessed by MTT assay and the obtained results are shown in Fig. 9. We have found that all nanofibrous scaffolds exhibited high cell viability, surpassing the control. The highest cell viability $(190\pm21.7\%)$ was observed in the case of CS/PVA nanofibers. Among them, the CS/PVA-T nanofibers showed the lowest cell viability of $96.1\pm6.6\%$ survival. This could be attributed to the crosslinking between TA and CS/PVA through hydrogen bonding that leads to a decrease in some active sites for cell interaction. Similarly, Aliabadi et al. [58] have shown that TA as a crosslinker results in lower cell viability. The toxic effect has even been shown with increasing TA content. The nanofibrous scaffolds containing ZIF nanoparticles displayed higher cell viability compared to control and TA-modified CS/PVA nanofibers. The results of the MTT assay suggested that the fabricated MOF-based nanofibrous scaffolds have good biocompatibility and were promising for further biomedical ap­ plications such as wound dressing. This result is in accordance with reported findings [59] indicating that ZIF-8 modified membrane exhibit good cell attachment and proliferation. Indeed, the incorporation of ZIF nanoparticles improved the hydrophilicity of the CS/PVA-T nanofibrous scaffolds, which helped the cells to attach and proliferate [60].
## 3.6. Antibacterial activity of nanofibrous scaffolds
The MIC values of nanofibrous scaffolds was determined to figure out the effective concentration of nanofibrous scaffolds against S. aureus and
E. coli bacteria. MICs of different concentrations of CS/PVA, CS/PVA-T, and CS/PVA-TZ nanofibrous scaffolds were determined (Table 4). The MIC values of CS/PVA for S. aureus and $E$ . coli were 6 and $8\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , respectively.
The disk diffusion assay was also carried out against S. aureus and E. coli to assess the antibacterial activity of nanofibrous scaffolds. Fig. 10a, b shows the inhibition zone of S. aureus and $E$ . coli growth caused by CS/PVA, CS/PVA-T, CS/PVA-Z, and CS/PVA-TZ and CS/PVA${\mathrm{TZ}}\ @\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{IF}$ nanofibrous scaffolds. It can be seen that all scaffolds exhibited antibacterial activity. As we expected, the incorporation of TA and ZIF-8 to CS/PVA nanofibrous scaffolds showed an enhanced antibacterial ac­ tivity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria compared with CS/PVA nanofibers. However, against Gram-positive S. aureus bacterium, they had a high antibacterial ability. The results indicated that the nanofibrous scaffolds containing TA and ZIF-8 were efficient in inhibiting the growth of bacteria and enhanced further as the concen­ tration of ZIF-8 nanocrystals increased up to $3\mathrm{wt\%}$ (CS/PVA-TZ3). The diameter of the inhibitory zone (Fig. 10c, d) of the CS/PVA-TZ3 nano­ fibrous scaffold against S. aureus and $E$ . coli bacteria were $13.02\pm1.38$ mm and $11.26\pm1.05\mathrm{cm}$ , respectively. The major contribution of ZIF-8 nanocrystals to kill bacteria is benefiting from their inherent antibac­ terial property [37]. Indeed, the positively charged $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions and 2-MIM ligand are responsible for antibacterial activity via destroying the mi­ crobial membrane.
## 4. Conclusions
A novel biocompatible hemostatic wound dressing via incorporation of ZIF-8 nanocrystals within and onto electrospun chitosan-based nanofibrous scaffolds was fabricated. ZIF-8 nanoparticles with an average size of about $60\mathrm{nm}$ and a high specific surface area $(851\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ were synthesized and incorporated within the TA-modified CS/PVA nanofibers. In-situ growth of ZIF-8 nanocrystals onto nanofibrous scaf­ folds also revealed uniform and continuous decoration throughout the nanofibers. The fabricated scaffolds exhibit good hydrophilicity and swelling as well as rapid hemostatic performance. The hydrophilic and porous structure of nanofibrous scaffolds with high specific surface area ZIF-8 not only favor rapid absorption of blood, but also the released positively charged zinc ions could electrostatically interact with red blood cells as well as initiate the coagulation pathway. The PT and aPTT assays suggested that the coagulation pathway of nanofibrous scaffolds was extrinsic blood coagulation. The nanofibrous scaffolds displayed excellent biocompatibility and also antibacterial activity against E. coli and S. aureus. The rapid hemostatic ability, antibacterial activity, and biocompatibility of developed ZIF-8 modified nanofibrous scaffolds as well as their appropriate hydrophilicity and mechanical properties suggest a promising strategy to develop novel and highly efficient he­ mostatic wound dressings.
